.\" Manual page for uniwidged. Written by hand rather than generated: the
.\" interesting half of this program is what it refuses, and a generator emits
.\" the flags and none of the reasons.
.TH UNIWIDGED 1 "2026-08-31" "uniwidged 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
uniwidged \- run sandboxed widgets on the desktop layer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uniwidged
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uniwidged
is one process that owns one GPU device and every widget's surface. Widgets sit
below ordinary windows and above the wallpaper, composited against it per pixel.
They are WebAssembly components: sandboxed, installable while the desktop is
running, and written in any language that targets the component model.
.PP
A widget can do nothing it was not granted. Everything it may do is written in
its manifest, shown to you before it is installed, and enforced by the host at
every call \(em not by the widget's good manners.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Running
.TP
.B run " [--config " \fIPATH\fR ]
Start the desktop in the foreground. One process, one device, one loop; an idle
desktop submits nothing at all.
.TP
.B doctor
What this session can and cannot do: the window system, the transparency path in
use, the fonts, and the keychain this machine can offer.
.TP
.B status
What is on the screen right now, which is a different question from what
\fBlist\fR answers: a widget can be enabled in the file and not on the screen
because its component was deleted, because its manifest no longer parses, or
because the desktop gave up on it after three failures. The running desktop
writes what it managed to do; this reads it, and says which shell the session
gave it and where each widget actually is.
.TP
.B log " [-n \fIN\fR] [-f]"
What the running desktop has been saying. A desktop started with the session
has no terminal, so everything it prints goes to a file beside the
configuration as well; this shows the last \fIN\fR lines (forty by default),
and with \fB-f\fR keeps showing them as they arrive, until the desktop stops.
The file is capped at a megabyte and rotated once, so it holds at most two.
.TP
.B reload
Tell a running desktop to read its configuration again. Every command that
writes the configuration already does this; \fBreload\fR is for a file you
edited by hand. A widget that has appeared is started, one that has gone or
been switched off is stopped, one whose version has changed is replaced, and
the rest are reconfigured and moved where the file says \(em all without
restarting the desktop.
.SS Installing
.TP
.B check " \fIwidget\fR.wasm"
Everything \fBinstall\fR would find out, without installing anything: the
manifest, the signature, the capabilities it will ask for, and the settings it
declares.
.TP
.B install " \fIwidget\fR.wasm [--allow-unsigned]"
Install a widget from a file, after showing what it asks for. A widget with a
signature that does not verify is never installed; one with no signature at all
needs \fB--allow-unsigned\fR, which says so out loud.
.TP
.B list " [--names]"
What is installed, where it sits, and what it was granted \(em and, while a
desktop is running, whether each one is actually on the screen. With
\fB--names\fR, one name per line and nothing else, for a script or a shell
completion.
.TP
.BR enable ", " disable " \fIname\fR"
Turn a widget off without forgetting it, and on again.
.TP
.B remove " \fIname\fR [--purge]"
Uninstall. With \fB--purge\fR, its data directory goes too, and so does the
copy in the widget store if it came from a registry. A widget installed from a
file somewhere else is left where its owner put it.
.SS Registries
.TP
.B registry add " \fIurl\fR --key \fIpublic key\fR [--name \fIname\fR]"
Read a registry, pinned to the key its index is signed with. That key says who
publishes the list; it is not, and must not be, the same thing as trusting
whoever wrote the widgets on it.
.TP
.BR "registry remove" " \fIname\fR, " "registry list"
Stop reading one, or show what is read and how old each cached index is.
.TP
.B registry publish " --key \fIfile\fR --base-url \fIurl\fR [--out \fIfile\fR] \fIwidget\fR.wasm..."
Write a signed index for widgets you publish.
.TP
.B search " [\fIterm\fR] [--refresh] [--offline]"
What the registries offer, with the permissions each widget will ask for.
.TP
.B add " \fIname\fR[@\fIversion\fR] [--registry \fIname\fR]"
Install one from a registry. The download is checked against the digest the
signed index names, and then against this machine's own trusted keys \(em a
registry cannot vouch a widget past them.
.TP
.B update " [\fIname\fR] [--check]"
What is newer, and take it. An update that would ask for more than the installed
version was granted is reported and \fInot\fR applied; \fBadd\fR is where that
decision is made, with the whole list shown.
.SS Signing
.TP
.B keygen " [--out \fIfile\fR]"
A signing key of your own. Anybody who has the secret half can publish widgets
in your name.
.TP
.B sign " \fIwidget\fR.wasm --key \fIfile\fR"
Sign a widget you publish. The signature covers the component
.I and
its manifest: one over the component alone would let anybody widen what the
widget may do and keep a signature your users recognise.
.TP
.BR trust " \fIkey\fR [--name \fIname\fR], " untrust " \fIkey\fR, " trusted
Whose signatures this machine accepts. One file, one key per line, meant to be
read by the person it protects.
.SS Placing and configuring
.TP
.B move " \fIname\fR [--x \fIN\fR] [--y \fIN\fR] [--width \fIN\fR] [--height \fIN\fR] [--output \fINAME\fR]"
Place a widget without a pointer. Anything omitted is left alone.
.TP
.BR lock " \fIname\fR, " unlock " \fIname\fR"
Pin a widget where it is, or let it be dragged again. A locked widget still
works; it just does not move.
.TP
.B configure " \fIname\fR [--set \fIkey\fR=\fIvalue\fR] [--reset \fIkey\fR] [--reset-all]"
The knobs a widget declared. A value outside what its manifest allows is refused
here, so a widget never has to handle one.
.SS Authoring
.TP
.B new " \fIname\fR [--dir \fID\fR] [--capability \fIC\fR]..."
Scaffold a widget that already builds, draws and responds to a click.
.TP
.B snapshot " \fIname\fR [-o \fIfile\fR]"
Render one widget headlessly to a PNG.
.SS Sessions
.TP
.B autostart " [enable|disable|status]"
Start with the session. It will not overwrite an autostart entry somebody wrote
by hand.
.TP
.B completions " bash|zsh|fish"
A completion script for that shell, written by this binary from the commands it
actually has. Widget names complete from what is installed. The packages install
these; from a source build, redirect the output to wherever the shell loads
completions from.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/uniwidged/config.toml
What is installed, where each widget sits, and what it was granted. Readable and
hand-editable on purpose.
.TP
.I ~/.config/uniwidged/trusted-keys
The keys this machine accepts signatures from. One per line.
.TP
.I ~/.config/uniwidged/registries.toml
The registries this machine reads, each with the key its index is pinned to.
.TP
.I ~/.config/uniwidged/registry-cache/
The last index fetched from each registry, and its signature. A cache: deleting
it costs one fetch, and it is verified again every time it is read.
.TP
.I ~/.config/uniwidged/store/
Widgets downloaded from a registry.
.TP
.I ~/.config/uniwidged/widgets/\fIname\fR/
One widget's own data directory. It cannot leave this, even through a symlink.
.TP
.IR ~/.config/uniwidged/uniwidged.log ", " uniwidged.log.1
What the running desktop said, with a timestamp on each line and \fB!\fR
before the ones that went wrong. Read with \fBlog\fR.
.TP
.IR ~/.config/uniwidged/uniwidged.pid ", " uniwidged.state
Left by the running desktop: its process id, and what it is showing. Neither
is believed on its own \(em a desktop killed outright leaves both behind, so
every reader checks that the process is alive and is this program first.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B UNIWIDGED_CONFIG
The configuration file to use. Everything else \(em trusted keys, registries,
the cache, the keychain, widget data \(em is placed beside it, so this variable
moves a whole desktop's state at once. That is what lets two desktops run side
by side, and what keeps a test out of your real one.
.TP
.B UNIWIDGED_REGISTRY_CA
A PEM certificate authority to trust for registry fetches
.I instead of
the default roots. For a registry inside an organisation, under a certificate no
public trust store has heard of.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the command did what it said, and 1 with a sentence on standard error
when it did not.
.SH SEE ALSO
The architecture, the roadmap and the authoring guide, at
.UR https://github.com/j-pfalzgraf/uniwidged
.UE .
